  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What does a kangaroo rat eat?",
      "a": "Mostly dry seeds from desert plants, which it carries in fur-lined cheek pouches and stores in its burrow. It sometimes eats green plants and insects, which add a little water.",
      "asks": [
        "Where does a kangaroo rat get its water?",
        "Why do kangaroo rats have cheek pouches?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is metabolic water?",
      "a": "When cells burn food with oxygen to release energy, water is produced as a by-product. All animals make some; a kangaroo rat gets about 90 percent of its water this way.",
      "asks": [
        "Can you get water from food?",
        "How do cells make water?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does a kangaroo rat's nose save water?",
      "a": "Its nasal passages are long and cool. Warm, moist air breathed out cools as it passes through, so water condenses on the inside of the nose and is reabsorbed instead of lost.",
      "asks": [
        "How do desert animals save water when breathing?",
        "Why is a kangaroo rat's nose cool?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How do a kangaroo rat's kidneys save water?",
      "a": "Its kidneys have very long loops that pull water back into the body, making tiny amounts of extremely concentrated urine, several times more concentrated than the most concentrated human urine.",
      "asks": [
        "Why is kangaroo rat urine so concentrated?",
        "How do kidneys save water?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why is it called a kangaroo rat?",
      "a": "It hops on its big back legs and balances with a long tail, like a tiny kangaroo, though it is a rodent, not a rat or a marsupial. Hopping helps it zigzag away from snakes and owls.",
      "asks": [
        "Is a kangaroo rat related to kangaroos?",
        "How does a kangaroo rat escape predators?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Merriam's kangaroo rat can survive without drinking, getting about 90 percent of its water from metabolism of its food.",
